      "musical_cue": "Echoing, spacious piano and cello. The sound of a factory shutting down (power down noise).",
      "summary": "\"Lexington Lights Out\" is a ghost-town ballad. It chronicles the shutdown of a massive meatpacking plant in a rural community. The song explores the \"brittle\" nature of efficiency: when a single corporation controls the entire regional capacity, their decision to leave destroys the local economy overnight. It depicts the silence of the kill floor, the empty parking lots, and the rancher who suddenly has nowhere to take his herd. It is a mourning song for the death of rural economic independence.",
      "lyrics": "[Intro]\n[Verse 1]\nThe whistle used to blow at six a.m.\nCalling in the shift to work for them\nA thousand cars in the parking lot\nConcrete islands boiling hot\nBut the memo came on a Tuesday noon\nSaid the end was coming mighty soon\nThey crunched the numbers in a tower high\nAnd decided that the town would have to die.\n[Pre-Chorus]\nEfficiency is a cold machine.\nIt picks the bone and the pocket clean.\n[Chorus]\nLexington lights out.\nLock the heavy gate.\nSeal the rusty loading dock and leave it to its fate\nThe kill floor is quiet, the chains are hanging still\nThe wind is blowing lonely on the processing hill\nThey moved the operation south for a penny on the pound\nAnd left a hollow silence in the middle of the town.\nLexington lights out.\n[Verse 2]\nNow the rancher stands with a heavy herd\nWaiting on the buyer for a single word\nBut the trucks don't run to the local yard\nAnd the winter ground is freezing hard\nHe has got to ship them five hundred miles away\nPaying for the diesel every single day\nThe monopoly promised it would bring the gold\nBut they left us in the shivers of the prairie cold.\n[Bridge]\nNo jobs at the counter.\nNo trucks on the street.\nJust the ghost of the industry.\nAnd the smell of the meat.\n(Fading away.)\n[Chorus]\nLexington lights out.\nLock the heavy gate.\nSeal the rusty loading dock and leave it to its fate\nThe kill floor is quiet, the chains are hanging still\nThe wind is blowing lonely on the processing hill\nThey moved the operation south for a penny on the pound\nAnd left a hollow silence in the middle of the town.\nLexington lights out.\n[Outro]\nLast one out...\nTurn off the lights."
