      "musical_cue": "Warm, organic folk. Upright bass and acoustic guitar. A feeling of community and gathering.",
      "summary": "\"The Prime Act (Butcher's Prayer)\" shifts the sonic landscape from cold, industrial noise back to warm, organic folk. It tells the story of a \"bootleg\" butcher session in a barn\u2014not because the meat is unsafe, but because the regulations make it impossible to sell legally. The narrator and his neighbor decide to bypass the \"Big Four\" entirely, processing the animal themselves to feed their families. It is a quiet anthem for food sovereignty, arguing that the safest meat doesn't come from a factory, but from a neighbor you know by name.",
      "lyrics": "[Intro]\n[Verse 1]\nPull the truck around the back and shut the headlights down\nWe don't need the federal boys coming from the town\nI got a steer that ate the grass right here on the hill\nAnd you got a family that has got a hungry belly to fill\nThe law says I have to ship him three hundred miles away\nTo a factory in Kansas just to get the USDA\nBut that don't make no sense when you live across the road\nSo tonight we are gonna lighten up the heavy load.\n[Pre-Chorus]\nThey call it contraband.\nI call it dinner.\nIn a game this rigged, you gotta be a sinner to be a winner.\n[Chorus]\nI got the steer and you got the knife\nLet us cut the red tape and feed the life\nNo stamp on the package, no plastic on the tray\nJust the honest work of a butchering day\nPass the roast to the widow and the burger to the priest\nTonight we are gathering for the outlaw feast\nYeah the government says that it is a federal crime\nBut we are eating like kings on the Prime Act time.\n[Verse 2]\nMy granddaddy did it this way back in thirty two\nBefore the bureaucrat decided what we could not do\nSafe and sound and clean as a whistle in the jar\nBetter than the meat that traveled in a railroad car\nIt is civil disobedience with a boning knife in hand\nTaking back the power and the produce of the land\nSo sharpen up the steel and clear the table space\nWe are putting the smile back on the neighbor face.\n[Bridge]\nThey say it is for safety.\nBut we know it is for control.\nBecause when you buy from a neighbor...\nYou buy the trust.\nAnd you buy the soul.\n[Chorus]\nI got the steer and you got the knife\nLet us cut the red tape and feed the life\nNo stamp on the package, no plastic on the tray\nJust the honest work of a butchering day\nPass the roast to the widow and the burger to the priest\nTonight we are gathering for the outlaw feast\nYeah the government says that it is a federal crime\nBut we are eating like kings on the Prime Act time.\n[Outro]\nPass the plate.\nIt is good."
